\details{
\strong{Method}

With \eqn{A} the matrix of squared distances and \eqn{J} the centering
matrix, db-RDA (McArdle & Anderson 2001) analyzes Gower's centered matrix
\eqn{G = -\frac{1}{2}JAJ}. With \eqn{H} the projection onto the (centered)
constraints, after removing any conditions, the constrained axes are the
eigenvectors of \eqn{HGH}, and the unconstrained axes are those of
\eqn{(I-H)G(I-H)}. Non-Euclidean distances, such as Bray-Curtis, can give
negative eigenvalues. They are kept in the inertia, as \code{vegan::dbrda()}
does, but have no axes.

\code{vegan::dbrda()} forms \eqn{G} and \eqn{HGH} in full and computes all
\eqn{n} of their eigenvalues, which takes time proportional to \eqn{n^3}.
\code{dbrda()} instead projects \eqn{G} onto an orthonormal basis of the
constraints, which only needs a few products of \eqn{G} with vectors,
computed in parallel straight from \code{dm}, and a small eigenproblem with one
row per constraint column. Unconstrained axes are only computed when
needed, by block Lanczos iteration as in \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}.

\strong{Matching vegan}

\code{dbrda()} matches \code{vegan::dbrda()}. It does not match \code{vegan::capscale()}
or \code{phyloseq::ordinate(method = "CAP")} (which calls \code{capscale()}):
\code{capscale()} first removes the negative eigenvalues of non-Euclidean
distances, such as Bray-Curtis, so its eigenvalues and scores differ from
\code{dbrda()}'s. For Euclidean distances, the two agree.

Columns of the model matrix that are linear combinations of earlier ones
(aliased) are dropped with a message, as in \code{vegan}.

\strong{Scale}

Like \code{vegan::dbrda()}, \code{dbrda()} divides the distances by
\eqn{\sqrt{n-1}} when any of them is 4 or more ("mean squared
distances"). That divides \code{trace}, \code{eig}, \code{inertia} and \code{constrained_eig}
by \eqn{n-1}, but leaves \code{prop} unchanged. So for the same \code{dm},
\code{attr(dbrda(dm, ...), 'trace')} equals \code{attr(pcoa(dm), 'trace')} when every
distance is below 4 (e.g. Bray-Curtis or Jaccard), and equals it divided
by \eqn{n-1} otherwise (e.g. Euclidean distances between counts).

\strong{Axis signs and reproducibility}

An eigenvector's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return
mirror images of the same axis. \code{dbrda()} flips each axis so that its
largest score in absolute value is positive, and flips the biplot scores
and centroids with it. Results are identical from run to run and for any
number of \code{cpus}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination method (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, and \code{\link[=ordination]{ordination()}}) returns
the same kind of object: a plain numeric matrix with one row per sample
(named after the samples) and one column per axis (named \code{"PCoA1"},
\code{"NMDS1"}, etc.), so that it can go straight into \code{plot()} or a data
frame. Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, or \code{NA} for methods without them.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance that each axis explains,
or \code{NA} where that isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them.
}

\examples{
    d    <- bray(ex_counts)
    meta <- data.frame(
      row.names = rownames(ex_counts),
      treated   = c(TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE) )

